From: Mateusz Krawczuk <mateusz.krawczuk@cybrixsystems.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: tegra194: relax EQOS AXI outstanding request limits

The EQOS node inherits snps,write-requests = <1> from the original
Tegra186 submission.  dwc-qos-eth parses this as "N outstanding AXI
write requests" and programs N-1 into DMA_SYSBUS_MODE.WR_OSR_LMT, so
the RX DMA engine ends up limited to a SINGLE outstanding AXI write
(and 3 reads for TX).  Every RX buffer write must then complete before
the next is issued, making throughput strictly memory-latency-bound.
At the BPMP's conservative boot EMC frequency (no Tegra194 interconnect
provider exists in mainline, so the EQOS interconnect paths never
request bandwidth) the added latency causes RX FIFO overflow and, under
sustained load, DMA stalls and TX watchdog resets.

NVIDIA's own production driver disagrees with the DT: the L4T r35
nvethernet resource manager (nvethernetrm/osi/core/eqos_core.c,
eqos_configure_dma()) programs RD_OSR_LMT = WR_OSR_LMT = 31 (the full
5-bit field) plus BLEN8|BLEN16 on the very same T194 EQOS instance.

Mainline dwmac4 masks the OSR fields to 4 bits (GENMASK(27,24) /
GENMASK(19,16) in dwmac4_dma.h), so the largest value expressible via
DT is 16 (register value 15).  Use that for both directions.  The
burst-map <0x7> (BLEN4|8|16) already covers NVIDIA's BLEN8|BLEN16 and
is left unchanged.

Upstream-Status: Pending (candidate for upstream; values derived from
NVIDIA's L4T OSI programming of the same IP instance, capped to the
4-bit field mainline dwmac4 writes)
Signed-off-by: Mateusz Krawczuk <mateusz.krawczuk@cybrixsystems.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/nvidia/tegra194.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/nvidia/tegra194.dtsi
@@ -159,8 +159,8 @@
 			iommus = <&smmu TEGRA194_SID_EQOS>;
 			status = "disabled";

-			snps,write-requests = <1>;
-			snps,read-requests = <3>;
+			snps,write-requests = <16>;
+			snps,read-requests = <16>;
 			snps,burst-map = <0x7>;
 			snps,txpbl = <16>;
 			snps,rxpbl = <8>;
